{
  "example": "Running `habeas unknown` fails with a nonzero exit status and prints a usage message. {cite}`requirements.cli.invocation`",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The example is covered by a test that sets up its scenario and checks its outcome.\n\n**Test.** `tests/cli.rs`, `fn unknown_command_fails_with_usage` (lines 31–41), is marked `{test}` for both `reference.cli.unknown_command` and `requirements.cli.invocation`. It runs the built binary (`CARGO_BIN_EXE_habeas`) with the single argument `unknown`. It then asserts two things:\n- `!output.status.success()` (the exit status is nonzero), and\n- stderr contains `Usage: habeas` (a usage message is printed).\n\nThese match the example's two expected outcomes: nonzero exit and a usage message.\n\n**Implementation.** `src/main.rs` defines `Cli` with `#[derive(Parser)]` and `#[command(name = \"habeas\")]`, with a required `Command` subcommand enum. `main` calls `Cli::parse()` first, before any workspace configuration loading. Clap rejects an unrecognized subcommand with exit status 2 and prints `error: unrecognized subcommand 'unknown'` followed by `Usage: habeas <COMMAND>` on stderr. That output is consistent with the test's assertion. I did not run the test or the binary.\n\n**Scope.** The test reaches only the clap parse path, which is all this example describes.",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "reference.cli.unknown_command",
      "matches": [
        "reference.cli.unknown_command"
      ],
      "text": [
        ":::{example} cli.unknown_command",
        "Running `habeas unknown` fails with a nonzero exit status and prints a usage message. {cite}`requirements.cli.invocation`",
        ":::"
      ]
    },
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.cli.invocation",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.cli.invocation"
      ],
      "text": [
        ":::{req} invocation",
        "`habeas mdbook supports html` MUST succeed, and a support query for another renderer MUST fail. An unknown command or invalid setup option combination MUST fail with a usage message. In particular, `habeas agents` and `habeas setup --install-skills` MUST fail with a usage message.",
        ":::"
      ]
    },
